  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nEarlier showers and thunderstorms have since died out in the\ngrowing surface inversion post sunset. Rain-free conditions are\nexpected to remain through Monday morning. An upper ridge will\ncontinue to build in the meantime with 500 mb heights reaching\n594+ dm through Monday afternoon. Temperatures will also continue\nto climb with afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s with\ndewpoints in the 70s. Therefore, heat indices will start to climb\nabove 105 F, most prominently in portions of far West TN,\nnorthwest MS, the MO Bootheel, and eastern AR where a Heat\nAdvisory remains in effect for Monday afternoon. Areas outside of\nthe Heat Advisory are still expected to see dangerous heat with\ntriple digit heat indices. Some showers and thunderstorms are\npossible (20% - 30% chance) as diurnally driven MLCAPE rises\nabove 2500 J/kg. Storms may be capable of locally damaging wind\ngusts and heavy downpours, as well as providing relief from the\nheat.\n\nThe upper ridge will settle in over the southern CONUS through\nthe rest of the week. Rain chances are forecast to decrease with\ntime, but high temperatures will remain steady in the middle to\nupper 90s with high humidity. So, expect heat indices in the\ntriple digits each afternoon with areas closer to, and west of,\nthe MS River most likely to experience 105+ F. Therefore,\nadditional heat headlines are expected to be needed through this\nweek. Afternoon showers and thunderstorms are still possible,\nespecially given the poor performance of CAMs beyond 24 hours,\nwhich currently show isolated to no convection Tuesday. With this\nin mind, will wait to issue any more heat headlines until future\ndates become better resolved with respect to afternoon\nconvection.\n\nWhile past forecast guidance has suggested that northwesterly\nflow may reach the region next weekend, ensembles now paint a\ndifferent picture. Zonal flow, which is currently across the\nGreat Lakes and northern Plains, will continue through the end of\nthe week. With time, the new guidance now shows this flow\nbecoming more northwesterly over the Northeast. This shift east\nnow places the Mid-South squarely under a more amplified ridge\nand would imply that temperatures may increase further than what\nwe are seeing to start this week. LREF probabilities of heat\nindices of 105 F or greater already approach 50% or higher next\nweekend support this new forecast trend. Another consequence of\nstronger ridging would be a drier forecast with PoPs struggling\nto get above 10% areawide starting as early as Thursday. In\nsummary, a hot, summer-like forecast is in the works for this\nweek and through next weekend with limited chances for afternoon\nrelief.",
